### A YANG Model for BGP-LS, BGP-LS-VPN, and BGP-LS-SPF
- **Presentation**: [01. A YANG Model for BGP-LS, BGP-LS-VPN, and BGP-LS-SPF](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lsvr-01-a-yang-model-for-bgp-ls-bgp-ls-vpn-and-bgp-ls-spf-00)
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-lsvr-bgp-ls-yang`
- **Presenter**: Mahesh Jethandani
- **Discussion**:
  - Mahesh Jethandani presented updates in version -05 of `draft-ietf-lsvr-bgp-ls-yang`.
  - Added RIB support (`loc-rib`, `adj-rib-in`, and `adj-rib-out`) for all three address families. Routes are keyed by encoded NLRI with decoded descriptors.
  - Updated the security considerations section following the RFC 8407 template.
  - Required a fix in the base BGP YANG model (`draft-ietf-idr-bgp-model`) regarding unprefixed leafref paths used in RIB groupings, which has been updated to version -22.
  - Remaining action items for the authors before requesting Working Group Last Call (WGLC) include adding EPN NLRI support and handling the compound key for label index format.
  - Ketan Talaulikar suggested not holding up the base YANG model to include every new feature (e.g., Flex-Algo TLVs), recommending instead that future BGP-LS extension specifications define their own YANG augments. Mahesh Jethandani agreed with drawing the line after the pending items.
  - Sue Hares noted coordination between IDR and LSVR for IANA assignments and YANG model progression.

### Proposed Update to BGP Link-State SPF NLRI Selection Rules
- **Presentation**: [02. Proposed Update to BGP Link-State SPF NLRI Selection Rules](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lsvr-02-proposed-update-to-bgp-link-state-spf-nlri-selection-rules-00)
- **Presenter**: Jie Dong
- **Discussion**:
  - Discussed optimization of NLRI selection rules defined in RFC 9552 Section 6.1 to avoid unnecessary/redundant update advertisements and improve convergence times.
  - Proposed modifications:
    - Rule 1: Provide a configuration knob to disable preferring self-originated NLRIs from directly connected peers.
    - Rule 3: Add an option (3b) to retain the existing NLRI when sequence numbers match, preventing duplicate advertisements.
    - Rule 4: Add BGP peer address comparison when candidate routes originate from the same peer.
  - Simulation results across various network topologies demonstrated reductions in generated updates and improved convergence times.
  - AC Lindem commented on edge cases like cold restarts of isolated routers, noting that non-volatile storage for sequence numbers helps avoid issues, and supported moving toward adoption.
  - The authors requested Working Group adoption for the draft.

### Applying BGP-LS Segment Routing over IPv6 (SRv6) Extensions to BGP-LS-SPF
- **Presentation**: [03. Applying BGP-LS Segment Routing over IPv6(SRv6) Extensions to BGP-LS-SPF](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lsvr-03-applying-bgp-ls-segment-routing-over-ipv6srv6-extensions-to-bgp-ls-spf-00)
- **Presenter**: Li Zhang
- **Discussion**:
  - Updates in version -08 include setting the Protocol ID to 4 (Direct), referencing RFC 9552 for SID NLRI procedure details, and adding SPF Status and Sequence Number TLVs.
  - AC Lindem suggested explicitly detailing what gets installed in the forwarding table and under what reachability conditions rather than leaving it implied, and mentioned considering support for micro-SIDs (uSIDs) in future revisions.
  - Authors requested Working Group adoption.

### Area-Scoped Link-State Distribution for BGP-LS-SPF
- **Presentation**: [04. Area-Scoped Link-State Distribution for BGP-LS-SPF](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lsvr-04-area-scoped-link-state-distribution-for-bgp-ls-spf-00)
- **Presenter**: Ximiao
- **Discussion**:
  - Proposed introducing logical areas into BGP-LS-SPF to contain local link-state updates within an area while maintaining global reachability via prefix advertisements through boundary routers.
  - Target applications include large, highly dynamic flat networks such as LEO satellite constellations.
  - AC Lindem noted similarities to OSPF alternate ABR concepts, emphasizing that loop prevention across arbitrary area structures (such as rings of areas) is complex and requires careful protocol design.
  - Ketan Talaulikar recommended consulting network operators deploying BGP-LS-SPF to confirm operational requirements before developing detailed solutions.

### Region Division in Networks using BGP-LS SPF Routing
- **Presentation**: [05. Region Division in Networks using BGP-LS SPF Routing](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lsvr-5-region-division-in-networks-using-bgp-ls-spf-routing-00)
- **Presenter**: Min
- **Discussion**:
  - Presented a problem statement and framework for dividing large flat BGP-LS-SPF networks (such as satellite networks) into regions without requiring a traditional backbone region.
  - Defines internal nodes and border nodes to scope flooding domains and prevent inter-region advertisement loops.
  - Li Zhang asked if any protocol extensions are proposed; Min clarified that no extensions are defined in the initial draft, but may be added based on WG interest.
  - AC Lindem supported using the term "region" to distinguish this concept from standard IGP areas and raised questions regarding how inter-region links and paths are represented.
  - Ketan Talaulikar and Jie Dong reiterated the need to gather operator requirements and coordinate across routing working groups (e.g., RTGWG) prior to standardizing solutions.

